</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We apply dimension-wise learning rates for all methods, as gradient magnitudes vary between <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{a},\bm{f},\bm{l},\bm{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒍</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{a},\bm{f},\bm{l},\bm{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math>.
FlowMM outputs <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{a}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{a}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 7}</annotation></semantics></math>, a soft 7-bit encoding that gets rounded to discrete values <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math>.
ALIGNN expects integer atomic numbers <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th atom to create atom embeddings <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by selecting the <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>-th row of its embedding matrix <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{E}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To maintain differentiability for D-Flow and PnP-Flow, we use the continuous values <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{k}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{k}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, derived from <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{a}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{a}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, to create soft atom embeddings <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle z_{ij}=(j-\tilde{k}_{i})^{2},\quad\bm{v}_{i}=\mathrm{softmax}(-\bm{z}_{i}/\tau),\quad\bm{h}_{i}=\mathbf{E}^{\mathsf{T}}\bm{v}_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>softmax</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐄</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msup><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z_{ij}=(j-\tilde{k}_{i})^{2},\quad\bm{v}_{i}=\mathrm{softmax}(-\bm{z}_{i}/\tau),\quad\bm{h}_{i}=\mathbf{E}^{\mathsf{T}}\bm{v}_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where we set <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>.
For DAPS, we avoid this approximation by using Langevin Monte Carlo only for <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{f},\bm{l},\bm{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒍</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{f},\bm{l},\bm{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math> and sampling <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math> with Metropolis–Hastings using proposals that flip one bit of the binarized atomic numbers.
The noising and denoising steps are adapted from the original diffusion formulation to match FlowMM.
Hyperparameter details are provided in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We measure performance using the absolute errors between sample properties and targets for conditional generation, and the predicted energy above hull for stability, as shown in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Material generation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ ESS-Flow: Training-free guidance of flow-based models as inference in source space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
Even with the continuous approximation for <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, D-Flow fails to explore atomic compositions far from initialization.
PnP-Flow, which optimizes in data space, is less restricted by this limitation.
By avoiding gradient-based exploration of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, DAPS and ESS-Flow perform better, specifically ESS-Flow outperforms all other methods significantly with the lowest errors.
The approximation quality is further illustrated in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.1 Material generation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ ESS-Flow: Training-free guidance of flow-based models as inference in source space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, where we see that ESS-Flow can better recover the sharp target distributions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Mean and standard deviation (in parentheses) of absolute errors between the sample properties and targets, and mean and standard deviation of energy above hull values.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Method</th>
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Bulk modulus</th>
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Shear modulus</th>
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">Band gap</th>
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Energy above hull</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Unconditional</th>
<td id="S5.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">198.37 (66.44)</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">166.71 (22.51)</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">9.44 (0.98)</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">2.19 (1.42)</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">D-Flow</th>
<td id="S5.T2.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">193.86 (69.54)</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">158.91 (35.13)</td>
